C = [
("h1", "3. Isaiah: The Wife Forsaken and Restored"),
("p", "Claim: Isaiah carries the figure through three stages. The mother is put away for her iniquities (Isaiah 50:1), the forsaken wife is called to sing because her Maker is her husband (Isaiah 54:1–8), and Zion is given a new name and is married to the one who rejoices over her (Isaiah 62:1–5). The wife is Zion and Jerusalem. The Gentiles stand beside the scene and see it. The prophet never gives the wife’s place to anyone else."),
("sub", "3.1 The mother who is put away"),
("p", "The first stage is a question put to a nation: “Where is the bill of your mother’s divorcement, whom I have put away? or which of my creditors is it to whom I have sold you?” (Isaiah 50:1). The LORD then answers it himself: “for your iniquities have ye sold yourselves, and for your transgressions is your mother put away.” Readers differ on how the opening question is to be taken, and the volume need not settle that. The verse shows what matters here: the nation is spoken of as the children of a mother, the mother is the wife, and the separation is spoken of in the language of divorce and is laid to the nation’s account."),
("sub", "3.2 “Sing, O barren”"),
("p", "The second stage is the fifty-fourth chapter, and it opens with a command to sing: “Sing, O barren, thou that didst not bear; break forth into singing, and cry aloud, thou that didst not travail with child: for more are the children of the desolate than the children of the married wife, saith the LORD” (Isaiah 54:1). The woman is desolate, and her husband is named a few verses later: “For thy Maker is thine husband” (Isaiah 54:5). He describes the interval in the language of a marriage that was broken and mended: “For the LORD hath called thee as a woman forsaken and grieved in spirit, and a wife of youth, when thou wast refused” (Isaiah 54:6)."),
("p", "The length of the interval and the nature of the return are stated in a single pair of verses: “For a small moment have I forsaken thee; but with great mercies will I gather thee. In a little wrath I hid my face from thee for a moment; but with everlasting kindness will I have mercy on thee” (Isaiah 54:7–8). The one who forsakes is the one who gathers, and the wife of youth is the wife who is gathered. The seed of the restored wife is Israel’s seed, and the nations are the field of its inheritance: “thy seed shall inherit the Gentiles, and make the desolate cities to be inhabited” (Isaiah 54:3). The chapter is not a transfer of the wife’s name. It is a restoration of the wife to her husband."),
("sub", "3.3 The new name"),
("p", "The third stage is the sixty-second chapter, and it names the woman. The prophet will not rest “for Zion’s sake” and “for Jerusalem’s sake” until the righteousness and salvation of the city go forth (Isaiah 62:1). The Gentiles and the kings are the ones who see: “the Gentiles shall see thy righteousness, and all kings thy glory: and thou shalt be called by a new name, which the mouth of the LORD shall name” (Isaiah 62:2). The new name is then given in two pairs: “Thou shalt no more be termed Forsaken; neither shall thy land any more be termed Desolate: but thou shalt be called Hephzibah, and thy land Beulah: for the LORD delighteth in thee, and thy land shall be married” (Isaiah 62:4)."),
("p", "The marriage follows in the next verse, and it moves between the city and her people in a way that bears on Revelation 21: “For as a young man marrieth a virgin, so shall thy sons marry thee: and as the bridegroom rejoiceth over the bride, so shall thy God rejoice over thee” (Isaiah 62:5). The wife is the city and the land, and the sons are the people who dwell in her. The figure does not pause to distinguish them, because in Isaiah the city and her inhabitants are one reality named in two ways. The bridegroom is “thy God,” the LORD of Isaiah 54:5. The Gentiles and kings watch."),
("tbl", "The table follows the three stages and the garments of the bride.", [
    ("Isa 50:1", "“for your iniquities have ye sold yourselves, and for your transgressions is your mother put away”", "The separation is laid to the nation’s account"),
    ("Isa 54:1", "“more are the children of the desolate than the children of the married wife”", "The desolate wife is called to sing"),
    ("Isa 54:5", "“For thy Maker is thine husband; the LORD of hosts is his name”", "The husband is the LORD of hosts"),
    ("Isa 54:7", "“For a small moment have I forsaken thee; but with great mercies will I gather thee”", "The forsaking is brief, the gathering is great"),
    ("Isa 62:2", "“the Gentiles shall see thy righteousness, and all kings thy glory”", "The nations see the restoration"),
    ("Isa 62:4", "“thou shalt be called Hephzibah, and thy land Beulah”", "The new names replace Forsaken and Desolate"),
    ("Isa 62:5", "“as the bridegroom rejoiceth over the bride, so shall thy God rejoice over thee”", "The bridegroom is thy God, and the bride is Zion"),
    ("Isa 61:10", "“as a bridegroom decketh himself with ornaments, and as a bride adorneth herself with her jewels”", "The garments of salvation and of righteousness are the bridal dress"),
  ], [0.14, 0.52, 0.34]),
("sub", "3.4 The garments"),
("p", "Two other verses give the bridal figure its clothing. In the sixty-first chapter the speaker rejoices because the LORD “hath clothed me with the garments of salvation, he hath covered me with the robe of righteousness, as a bridegroom decketh himself with ornaments, and as a bride adorneth herself with her jewels” (Isaiah 61:10). In the forty-ninth chapter Zion is told that her returning children are her ornament: “thou shalt surely clothe thee with them all, as with an ornament, and bind them on thee, as a bride doeth” (Isaiah 49:18). The bride of Isaiah is dressed in righteousness and in her own people. The reader should keep both pictures in mind for Revelation 19:8."),
("sub", "3.5 What this chapter does not say"),
("bul", [
    "It does not say that Galatians 4:27, which quotes Isaiah 54:1, makes the church the wife. That passage is answered in Chapter 13, and the answer rests on what Paul says and what he does not say.",
    "It does not say that the Gentiles are shut out of blessing. It says that in these passages they see, and that the wife’s seed inherits the nations.",
    "It does not say that the city and the land and the sons are three different brides. The prophet speaks of one wife under the names that suit each part of the picture.",
  ]),
("call", "Where the argument stands", "Isaiah puts the divorce, the song and the new name in a single line of thought. The wife is Zion, her husband is her Maker and Redeemer, her new names are Hephzibah and Beulah, and the nations are the ones who see her restored."),
]
